resource: the placed-resource compression rule fires for committed resources too, and on Turnip that costs UBWC on every non-render-target image.

`resource.c:774-785` decides whether to give up on lossless bandwidth compression
for an image. Its comment is explicit about what it is protecting against:

    /* D3D12 rules for placed resources are such that only RTV / DSV usage
     * requires initialization. ... */

That is a real hazard and this patch does not dispute it. A *placed* resource
shares heap bytes with whatever else the application chooses to place there, and
D3D12 only obliges the application to initialize a placed resource when it has
RTV or DSV usage. Everything else is left uninitialized by contract, so a
compression metadata buffer belonging to one tenant of those bytes can be read or
written through another tenant's alias. Disabling compression is the pragmatic
answer, and upstream says so.

**The rule is applied far wider than its own justification reaches.** The test is

    if (!(desc->Flags & (ALLOW_DEPTH_STENCIL | ALLOW_RENDER_TARGET)))
        disable_compression = true;

which reads `desc->Flags` and nothing else. `resource->flags` is in scope — this
same function reads it four lines later at `:923` and again at `:1051` and
`:1127` — and it is never consulted here. So a committed resource, a placed
resource and a reserved resource are treated identically, and only one of the
three can alias.

**vkd3d has already decided this question, in its own code, the other way.**
`vkd3d_check_subresource_overlap` — the function that answers "can these two
resources be the same memory?" for aliasing barriers — opens with
(`libs/vkd3d/command.c:5525-5527`):

    /* No aliasing possible if either resource is committed */
    if ((a->flags | b->flags) & VKD3D_RESOURCE_COMMITTED)
        return false;

and the same file's next two branches say sparse can alias anything and placed
aliases by heap-range overlap. `vkd3d_get_image_create_info` agrees with itself
elsewhere too: `VK_IMAGE_CREATE_ALIAS_BIT` is granted at `:1051` only when the
resource is placed or absent, never when it is committed. The compression rule is
the one place in the file that ignores that answer.

The memory backs it up. A committed texture takes its size from
`vkGetImageMemoryRequirements2` on its own image (`resource.c:4213`) and binds
that image and no other to the result. When the allocator is allowed to
suballocate it, `resource.c:4300-4303` pads both alignment and size up to
`bufferImageGranularity` with the comment *"to prevent resource aliasing"*. There
is no neighbour inside a committed resource's range to clobber, and nothing to
inherit from a previous tenant.

## What it costs here

`disable_compression` has two consumers. One is `VkImageCompressionControlEXT`
(`:861-869`), which is the mechanism the code actually wants. The other is the
branch at `:805-835`, which is the fallback:

    image_info->flags |= VK_IMAGE_CREATE_MUTABLE_FORMAT_BIT;
    memset(compat_list, 0, sizeof(*compat_list));

**On this stack only the fallback ever runs.** Turnip does not implement
`VK_EXT_image_compression_control` — there is not one reference to it anywhere
under `native/mesa/src/freedreno/` — so
`d3d12_device_should_use_image_compression_control` (`:662-669`) is false and the
proper mechanism never engages. Mutable-format-with-no-list is the whole of what
happens.

And that shape is exactly what Turnip fails closed on.
`format_list_ubwc_possible` (`native/mesa/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_image.cc:497-519`)
opens with

    /* If there is no format list, we may have to assume that a
     * UBWC-incompatible format may be used.
     * TODO: limit based on compatiblity class
     */
    if (!fmt_list || !fmt_list->viewFormatCount)
       return false;

Its three sibling helpers — `format_list_reinterprets_r8g8_r16` (`:447`),
`format_list_has_swaps` (`:469`), `format_list_has_uncompressed_format` (`:486`)
— all fail *open*. This one does not. Adreno 830 is A8XX and inherits
`ubwc_all_formats_compatible = True` from `a7xx_gen3`
(`freedreno_devices.py:880`, `:1502-1506`), which puts it on the branch at
`tu_image.cc:658-662` where that fail-closed check is the *only* gate:

    } else {
       is_mutable = true;
       if (!format_list_ubwc_possible(device, fmt_list, pCreateInfo))
          ubwc_enabled = false;
    }

So every D3D12 image that is neither a render target nor a depth target is
created with UBWC off, on a part where it did not have to be, on a
bandwidth-bound tiler.

**It is silent.** The three `perf_debug` calls for mutable-format UBWC loss
(`tu_image.cc:618`, `:631`, `:640`) sit in the NV12 branch and the
`!mutable_ubwc_fc` legacy branch. The a8xx branch above has none, so
`TU_DEBUG=perf` reports nothing and never would have.

## Why the AMD and NVIDIA reasoning does not transfer

The hazard upstream is describing is a compression metadata **write**: a DCC or
HTILE initialization performed on the transition away from `UNDEFINED`, landing on
bytes a neighbouring alias owns. `resource.c:4608-4613` states it plainly for
placed render targets — *"extremely dangerous ... the initialization will clobber
other aliased buffers when clearing DCC/HTILE state"*.

**Turnip performs no such write.** The entire `UNDEFINED` handling in the barrier
path is a cache flush and nothing else
(`native/mesa/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_cmd_buffer.cc:9997-10011`):

       /* The underlying memory for this image may have been used earlier
        * within the same queue submission for a different image ... so we
        * need to flush the CCU. */
       if (vk_format_is_depth_or_stencil(image->vk.format)) {
          src_flags |= TU_ACCESS_CCU_DEPTH_INCOHERENT_WRITE;
       } else {
          src_flags |= TU_ACCESS_CCU_COLOR_INCOHERENT_WRITE;
       }

The render-pass equivalent (`tu_pass.cc:384-396`) does the same and its comment
says so: *"We currently only care about undefined layouts, because we have to
flush/invalidate CCU for those."* There is no metadata initialization anywhere in
the driver. The flag buffer is defined by the first full write to the image, and
every write path programs the destination flag-buffer base
(`tu_clear_blit.cc:528`, `:1575`, `:1583`).

**Turnip has the mirror of the problem instead, and it points the same way.** The
flag buffer is not written on transition, so a UBWC image placed over reused
memory *reads* the previous tenant's bytes as flag data. Turnip states this
itself, and its response is to refuse UBWC rather than to initialize anything
(`tu_image.cc:338-343`):

    /* UBWC isn't possible with sparse residency, because unbound blocks may
     * have leftover fast-clear data and therefore may show up as non-zero. */

That is a reason to leave placed resources exactly as they are — Turnip never
inspects `VK_IMAGE_CREATE_ALIAS_BIT` at all, so vkd3d's rule is the only thing
protecting a placed resource on this driver — and no reason whatsoever to
disable compression on a committed one.

## What the patch does and does not touch

Committed, non-shared resources are exempted. Everything else keeps the old
behaviour:

- **Placed** (`VKD3D_RESOURCE_PLACED`, `:4463`) — the case the rule was written
  for. Unchanged. Note this covers borrowed/external resources too, which
  `d3d12_resource_create_borrowed` flags as placed (`:4145`).
- **Reserved / sparse** (`VKD3D_RESOURCE_RESERVED`, `:4716`) — these carry
  `VK_IMAGE_CREATE_SPARSE_ALIASED_BIT` unconditionally (`:878-880`) and aliasing
  is the point of them. Unchanged, and Turnip would refuse UBWC anyway per the
  quote above.
- **The allocation-info query** (`vkd3d_get_image_allocation_info`, `:1233`,
  which passes `resource == NULL`). Unchanged, and this matters more than it
  looks: the query exists to size a heap for a placed resource, so it has to
  answer identically to the placed creation that follows or the application
  allocates a heap too small for the flag buffer. Treating `NULL` as placed is
  the same choice `:1051` and `:1127` already make.
- **`D3D12_HEAP_FLAG_SHARED`** — excluded, because the memory is exported and
  vkd3d does not control what the importer creates over it. It costs one term
  and removes the question.
- **The `VKD3D_CONFIG` overrides** (`:790-801`) run after this and still win, so
  `disable_uav_compression`, `disable_color_compression` and
  `disable_depth_compression` behave exactly as before.

Unconditional rather than driver-gated, on the 0004 and 0005 pattern. The
argument is not "Turnip is special" — it is that the code's own stated premise is
about placed resources and vkd3d's own aliasing predicate already excludes
committed ones. Upstream's comment concedes the AMD side is inert as it stands
(*"On RADV at least, compression is not enabled for pure STORAGE images anyway,
so this is mostly to avoid future regressions"*), and NVIDIA is excluded from the
compression-control mechanism by design at `:664-668`.

## Not measured, and it cannot be measured directly

**No number is claimed.** This device exposes no DRAM bandwidth or DDR clock
counter to an application or to the shell (`docs/BANDWIDTH.md` §3), so there is
no honest percentage to quote and none is given here.

The one working instrument is `MESA_GPU_TRACES=print_csv` with `MESA_LOG=file`,
whose `render_pass` record carries a per-attachment `ubwc` y/n
(`tu_cmd_buffer.cc:3001-3006`). **It reports attachments only**, which are by
construction the images this patch does not change — a render target already had
`ALLOW_RENDER_TARGET` and already kept its format list. So it cannot observe the
effect head-on. How to confirm it anyway is in `patches/vkd3d/README.md`.

## Relationship to `docs/BANDWIDTH.md` §4 candidate 3

That candidate names the castable-format truncation at `resource.c:373-381`
(*"Too many formats to expect compression, just use plain mutable"*) as the hole.
It was investigated and is unreachable: it requires an application to pass
`num_castable_formats` and a format family that fills
`VKD3D_MAX_COMPATIBLE_FORMAT_COUNT`. This is the common path instead, and it
needs no castable formats and no unusual format family — only a resource that is
not a render target. Candidate 3 should be closed and replaced by this.

## Not compiled

Generated against the working tree with `0001`-`0005` applied and verified with
`git apply --check` from inside `native/vkd3d`. No vkd3d build has been run
against it.

**Policy.** Vessel's patch on Vessel's build. Defensible upstream as written,
since it is a plain inconsistency between this rule and `vkd3d_check_subresource_overlap`,
but the performance case for it is Turnip's and has not been measured on any
other driver. AI-authored in full.

diff --git a/libs/vkd3d/resource.c b/libs/vkd3d/resource.c
--- a/libs/vkd3d/resource.c
